<td id="A4.T2.10.9.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A4.T2.10.9.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">.69<math id="A4.T2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\pm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\pm}</annotation></semantics></math>.08</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Variance reduction with control variates</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide additional justification for using control variates to reduce the variance of importance-weighted estimators in <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Importance-reweighted estimators of uncertainty ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Efficient semantic uncertainty quantification in language models via diversity-steered sampling" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 3.3</span></a>. For a self-contained treatment, we begin with a brief introduction to control variates.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\sim p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\sim p</annotation></semantics></math> be a random variable and assume we wish to estimate its moment</p>
<table id="A5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\;=\;\mathbb{E}_{p}\bigl[h(Z)\bigr]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\;=\;\mathbb{E}_{p}\bigl[h(Z)\bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">using <math id="A5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> iid samples <math id="A5.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z_{1:N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{1:N}</annotation></semantics></math>. The classical estimator takes the form</p>
<table id="A5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}\;=\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}h(Z_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}\;=\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}h(Z_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which has variance <math id="A5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Var}[\hat{\mu}]=\frac{\operatorname{Var}[h(Z)]}{N}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Var}[\hat{\mu}]=\frac{\operatorname{Var}[h(Z)]}{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The idea of control variates is to pick any auxiliary function <math id="A5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> whose mean
<math id="A5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{g}=\mathbb{E}_{p}[g(Z)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{g}=\mathbb{E}_{p}[g(Z)]</annotation></semantics></math> is known in closed form.
For any coefficient <math id="A5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}_{\text{cv}}(\alpha)\;=\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\Bigl\{h(Z_{i})-\alpha\bigl[g(Z_{i})-\mu_{g}\bigr]\Bigr\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>cv</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}_{\text{cv}}(\alpha)\;=\;\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\Bigl\{h(Z_{i})-\alpha\bigl[g(Z_{i})-\mu_{g}\bigr]\Bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">is unbiased, because <math id="A5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{p}[g(Z)-\mu_{g}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{p}[g(Z)-\mu_{g}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It then remains to choose the optimal coefficient <math id="A5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes the variance. Define
<math id="A5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{h}^{2}=\operatorname{Var}[h(Z)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{h}^{2}=\operatorname{Var}[h(Z)]</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{g}^{2}=\operatorname{Var}[g(Z)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{g}^{2}=\operatorname{Var}[g(Z)]</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="A5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{hg}=\operatorname{Cov}[h(Z),g(Z)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{hg}=\operatorname{Cov}[h(Z),g(Z)]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Writing <math id="A5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=h(Z)-\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=h(Z)-\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=g(Z)-\mu_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=g(Z)-\mu_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="A5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Var}\!\bigl[\hat{\mu}_{\text{cv}}(\alpha)\bigr]=\frac{1}{N}\operatorname{Var}\!\bigl[\,H-\alpha G\bigr]=\frac{1}{N}\bigl(\sigma_{h}^{2}-2\alpha\,\sigma_{hg}+\alpha^{2}\sigma_{g}^{2}\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.643em;" width="1.643em"><mi>Var</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>cv</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.643em;" width="1.643em"><mi>Var</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Var}\!\bigl[\hat{\mu}_{\text{cv}}(\alpha)\bigr]=\frac{1}{N}\operatorname{Var}\!\bigl[\,H-\alpha G\bigr]=\frac{1}{N}\bigl(\sigma_{h}^{2}-2\alpha\,\sigma_{hg}+\alpha^{2}\sigma_{g}^{2}\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Differentiating with respect to <math id="A5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and setting the result to zero,</p>
<table id="A5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial\alpha}\operatorname{Var}=0\;\implies\;-2\sigma_{hg}+2\alpha\sigma_{g}^{2}=0\;\implies\;\alpha^{\star}=\frac{\sigma_{hg}}{\sigma_{g}^{2}}=\frac{\operatorname{Cov}[h(Z),g(Z)]}{\operatorname{Var}[g(Z)]}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Var</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">⟹</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial\alpha}\operatorname{Var}=0\;\implies\;-2\sigma_{hg}+2\alpha\sigma_{g}^{2}=0\;\implies\;\alpha^{\star}=\frac{\sigma_{hg}}{\sigma_{g}^{2}}=\frac{\operatorname{Cov}[h(Z),g(Z)]}{\operatorname{Var}[g(Z)]}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Substituting <math id="A5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> back yields</p>
<table id="A5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Var}\!\bigl[\hat{\mu}_{\text{cv}}(\alpha^{\star})\bigr]=\frac{1}{N}\,\sigma_{h}^{2}\,(1-\rho^{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.643em;" width="1.643em"><mi>Var</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>cv</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Var}\!\bigl[\hat{\mu}_{\text{cv}}(\alpha^{\star})\bigr]=\frac{1}{N}\,\sigma_{h}^{2}\,(1-\rho^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p4.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{\sigma_{hg}}{\sigma_{h}\sigma_{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{\sigma_{hg}}{\sigma_{h}\sigma_{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the correlation between <math id="A5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>,
so variance is reduced by the factor <math id="A5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\rho^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\rho^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the closer <math id="A5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\rho|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\rho|</annotation></semantics></math> is to 1, the greater the reduction in variance.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Semantic entropy</h4>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The estimator proposed in <a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Semantic entropy. ‣ 3.4 Variance reduction via adaptive control variates ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Efficient semantic uncertainty quantification in language models via diversity-steered sampling" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation 14</span></a> uses</p>
<table id="A5.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{N}{\tilde{w}}_{i}X_{i}^{\prime}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{N}{\tilde{w}}_{i}X_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">as the control variate. The centered negative log probabilities <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}^{\prime}=X_{i}-\mu_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}^{\prime}=X_{i}-\mu_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}=-\log p(s^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}=-\log p(s^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math> are strongly correlated with the negative cluster probabilities <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}=-\log{\hat{p}}(c(s^{(i)}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}=-\log{\hat{p}}(c(s^{(i)}))</annotation></semantics></math>, being based on the same sample <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>. In natural language generation, likely samples with low <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> tend to be mapped to dominant clusters, so <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes more negative when <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is more negative.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed control variate does not incur extra inference cost, as the log probabilities were already computed for evaluating the importance weights <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{w_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{w_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Particularly in the context of self-normalized importance sampling, log probabilities behave nicer than, e.g., a function of the ratio <math id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p/q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p/q</annotation></semantics></math>, which can take higher moments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
